Report [ 9]. Finally, a discussion on the advantages and limitations
of our approaches is provided in Section 6.
5.1 Exposure Discovery
In the pre-processing phase, we determine the exposure of each
input token position to the LLM denoted as XL(𝑖). For this experi-
ment, we utilize two widely adopted closed-source LLMs: GPT-3.5
Turbo andGPT-4o Mini, and two open-source LLMs: Gemma-
2:27B [36] and Phi-4:14B [1]. To measure token exposure values,
as outlined in Section 4, we construct a synthetic task denoted as
(I,𝑞), where each element within Icorresponds to an individual
token. The query 𝑞prompts the LLM to report the frequency of
occurrence for each token in the input set I. The LLM’s output is
represented as a key-value list, where each key corresponds to a
token, and the associated value indicates the number of times that
token was identified within the input.
We systematically reposition various tokens, including their
repeated occurrences across consecutive positions (aka windows)
within the inputI, to assess the extent to which each position is
exposed to the LLM. We estimate the exposure levels by conducting
multiple sampling iterations, as illustrated in Figure 3. The errors
are computed as the absolute difference between the actual repeats
of each token and the corresponding LLM prediction, averaged
across all unique tokens. The exposure is modeled as the inverse of
error at each position.
Based on Figure 3, GPT-3.5 Turbo tends to focus more on the
beginning of a long prompt, while it is more likely to forget the later
tokens. In contrast, GPT-4o Mini shows a different pattern, where it
is more likely to forget tokens at the start and the end while keeping
those in the middle. As shown in the Technical Report [ 9], Phi-4
behaves similarly to GPT-4o Mini, mainly retaining the middle part
of the prompt. On the other hand, Gemma-2 remembers tokens at
the end of the prompt but tends to forget the beginning.
We leverage the exposures identified in this subsection as a
preprocessing step to address the subsequent tasks discussed in the
following subsection.
5.2 Tasks Setup
In this section, we present a detailed overview of the task setups
and the datasets utilized.
5.2.1 Publication Venue Task. Following Example 1, we utilized
theDBLP ACM dataset [ 29], with approximately 132,000 entries. This
dataset provides a comprehensive list of research papers, including
attributes such as titles, authors, and the journals in which the
papers were published. It is widely used for entity resolution tasks.
For our study, we extracted a smaller sample of rows from this
dataset. The task involved passing the sampled DataFrame to the
LLM as input and querying it with questions such as, "How many
papers were published in physics-related journals?" to determine
the number of papers relevant to a given research topic.
5.2.2 Graph Degree Task. In this task, we generate a random graph
using the Erdős-Rényi model [ 14], where the edges represent the
set of elements, denoted as I. The primary query 𝑞for this graph
is: “What is the degree of a given node 𝑣?”. We systematically
select various nodes 𝑣within the graph and evaluate the responses
provided by an LLM to these queries. Since the generated graph is
fully accessible, we can compute the ground-truth degree of eachqueried node. To assess the accuracy of the LLM’s responses, we
calculate the error 𝜀Las the absolute difference between the degree
reported by the LLM and the ground-truth degree. We first pass the
set of edges to the LLM and then ask the query in a different prompt
message but in the same context. We selected graph sizes up to 500
edges to generate synthetic graphs to ensure that all edges could
be represented within a single prompt when inputting them into
LLMs, thereby adhering to the models’ token limits.
5.2.3 Dataset Query Task. Similar to the publication venue task,
in this task, we provide a dataset table as input to the LLM and ask
aggregation queries in natural language. For instance, a query might
ask, "How many rows contain the value in the ‘col’ column equal
to ‘value’?"
We utilize three real-world datasets for this study. The IMDB
Movies dataset[ 26] provides information on the top 1,000 movies
listed on IMDB . We extract a subset containing 60 rows from this
dataset and formulate a query regarding the number of movies with
a rating of𝑅𝑎𝑡𝑖𝑛𝑔≥8.2. TheAdult Income dataset [ 2], which
comprises data on approximately 48,000 individuals for income
prediction tasks, is also employed. From this dataset, we sample 60
rows and pose a query about the number of individuals associated
with a specific “workclass” category. Finally, the Open University
Learning Analytics ( OULAD ) dataset [ 19] includes data on student
enrollments across various courses. It contains 32K rows. For our
task, we sample a subset of 100 rows and ask about the number
of students enrolled in a particular course. These sample sizes are
carefully selected to align with the token limits of LLMs4. The re-
sults for the Adults and OULAD are presented in the Technical
Report [9].
5.3 Analysis of the Ranking utility
In this subsection, we utilize different small-sizes open-source LLMs
as helpers (H) to estimate the relevance scores of elements to
the query (𝑅𝑒𝑙𝑞). We use five different models: DeepSeek-Coder-
v2:16B [43],Gemma2:9B [36],Llama3.1:8B [11],Qwen2:7B [41],
andMistral:7B [18]. This selection, while random within the range
of small models (approximately 7B–8B parameters), ensures cover-
age of diverse and widely used open-source models.
We compare four ranking methods in this experiment. The first
method, Warm-up, as described in Section 3.1, involves querying
each partition of the input and subsequently splitting it. The second
method, Bipartite, detailed in Section 3.2, utilizes Bipartite Graph
Modeling to estimate relevance scores. To establish a lower bound
for performance comparison, we use the Random method, which
involves randomly shuffling all the input elements within the set I
and passing them to the language model, L. We expect this method
to give us less accurate results. As an upper bound, we employ the
Optimum method, where elements are completely sorted based on
explicit knowledge of the task to maximize relevance to the query.
This approach provides a theoretical upper limit for ranking utility,
and we expect it to give us the best answer. For example, for the
