Appendix
A Related Work
In this section, we give a brief literature review for this work. A more
detailed literature review is provided in the Technical Report [9].
Handling Long Inputs: The challenge of handling long input
prompts is a widely recognized issue in large language models. Var-
ious studies have approached this problem from multiple perspec-
tives. One notable scenario occurs when prompts involve in-context
learning [ 4,5,40], which often results in extended inputs that LLMs
struggle to fully process. The “Lost in the Middle” phenomenon5,
as identified by Liu et al. [ 23], highlights this issue, where LLMs fail
to retain or utilize certain portions of lengthy inputs. Other studies
have approached this issue by modifying the training datasets used
for LLMs [ 16]. Several studies have explored modifying the archi-
tecture of LLMs, particularly the attention mechanisms, to improve
their ability to process extensive context and handle larger input
sizes [3, 7, 8, 17, 20, 42].
Debiasing LLM outputs: Part of our technical solution in Sec-
tion 3 involves estimating input elements’ relevance to the query,
where we learn a so-called bias coefficient for various LLM evalua-
tions. Debiasing LLM outputs is extensively studied across various
domains such as recommender systems rankings[ 6], using a variety
of solutions such as output aggregation [ 13], prompt-writing [ 12],
and many more [ 10,27,30,34,35,38]. Note that despite their name
similarity, the objective of these papers is different as they aim to
mitigate the societal bias and unfairnesses introduced during vari-
ous stages such as data-driven systems. Related work also includes
reducing the output variance of LLMs using self-consistency meth-
ods [ 37], where the diversity of the model’s responses is leveraged
to enhance reliability and accuracy. This approach is fundamentally
different from our relevance estimation subproblem, where we aim
to estimate the relevance of input items to the query.
